{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Elohim you shall not curse.</b> [secular context]" "<b>Elohim you shall not curse.</b> It was juxtaposed here because perhaps the lender will pass by and not return the pledge, and the poor man, out of great distress, will come at night and curse the judge who decreed that his garment be taken as pledge — and the judge acted according to law and committed no offense in the matter." "<b>And a nasi among your people you shall not revile.</b> Why is this said? If to warn not to curse the judge, it has already been said. Rather, an unspecified <b>nasi</b> means a wealthy person who lends to others, and it is juxtaposed to 'if you take your fellow's garment as pledge' — Scripture warns him not to curse the rich man whose garment he took as pledge, since he benefited him from his property."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Hebrew comment consists only of a lemma head with the bracketed gloss חול ('secular/profane'), suggesting this entry addresses the scope or context of the prohibition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:54.878150467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}